# Copy to `deployment/.env` and edit if a conventional port is already taken on this
# machine. Only the HOST side of each mapping changes; inside the compose network the
# services always reach each other on their standard ports, so nothing else moves.
#
# You do not have to work out which port is taken: `make doctor` names the holder and
# prints the exact line to add here. `make up` runs it first.
#
# POSTGRES_PORT=5432
# NEO4J_HTTP_PORT=7474
# NEO4J_BOLT_PORT=7687
# REDIS_PORT=6379
# BACKEND_PORT=8000
# FRONTEND_PORT=3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The API (module 16). These are NOT ports and are not optional.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# CAUSALOG_API_SECRET signs and verifies bearer tokens (ADR-0082). There is deliberately
# no default anywhere in the code: a default secret is a published secret, and an API that
# authenticates against one authenticates nobody. Generate a fresh value per deployment,
# at least 32 characters, e.g.:
#
#     python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
#
# CAUSALOG_API_SECRET=
#
# CAUSALOG_ENV decides how much of a failure a response may describe. Anything other than
# `production` returns the engine's own diagnostic message, which names the offending
# identifier and the contract violated; `production` replaces it with a fixed public
# sentence per error class. It DEFAULTS TO production when unset, because the failure modes
# are asymmetric: a production deployment that accidentally reveals internals is an
# incident, while a development box that accidentally hides them costs one variable.
#
# CAUSALOG_ENV=development
#
# CAUSALOG_REPOSITORY_ROOT is where ontology/, rule_engine/ and datasets/ are mounted.
# Derived from the installed package location when unset, which is a development
# convenience; a container that mounts the packs elsewhere must set it, or it will read
# packs nobody edited.
#
# CAUSALOG_REPOSITORY_ROOT=/app
